# Write up the research idea into a complete paper

Your goal is to write up the following idea into a complete scientific paper:

```markdown
The research overview is in `/workspace/materials/research_overview.md`; read it in full before starting.
```

Note that the overview represents a preliminary hypothesis and may not align
exactly with the experiments that were eventually performed.

First, refer to the experiment data in `/workspace/materials/tables/` and the
table descriptions in `/workspace/materials/table_summary.txt`.

Refer to the experiment figures in `/workspace/materials/figures/` and their
descriptions in `/workspace/materials/figure_summary.txt`.

To better understand the methodology and experiments, refer to
`/workspace/materials/code/` as the proposed method's code implementation.

Read `/workspace/materials/template.tex` as the starting point for a complete,
coherent, scientifically accurate paper. `template.tex` and
`/workspace/materials/references.bib` are read-only; do not modify either. The
result must be an acceptable complete LaTeX writeup for a 10-page single-column
paper.

Use citations from `/workspace/materials/references.bib` and report results
accurately based on the provided experimental data. `references.bib` can be very
large; do not read it all at once — use Grep to find relevant keys or authors,
then read only the portions you need.

Start by reading `template.tex`, then write the completed document to
`/workspace/submission/main.tex`.

For the bibliography, do not use the `\begin{filecontents}{references.bib}`
environment. All citations must refer to an external file named `references.bib`.
Copy `/workspace/materials/references.bib` unchanged to
`/workspace/submission/references.bib`.

## Benchmark instructions

You are an ambitious AI researcher publishing a paper that contributes
significantly to the field. This is a method paper; the main contribution is a
novel technique, algorithm, model, or framework addressing a specific research
problem.

Ensure the paper is scientifically accurate, objective, and truthful. Report the
experimental results accurately, even if negative or inconclusive. You are
submitting to a top-tier conference, whose guidelines require:

- Use the available space and include all relevant information.
- Keep the mandated style and the external `references.bib` method.
- Copy referenced figure assets from `/workspace/materials/figures/` to
  `/workspace/submission/figures/`, preserving subdirectories, and reference them
  relative to the submission root (e.g. `\includegraphics{figures/foo.png}`).
- Do not add an `Acknowledgements` section.
- Use one backslash for LaTeX commands (`\section`, not `\\section`). Standard
  table row endings (`\\`) are allowed.

Compile from `/workspace/submission/`; the verifier recompiles `main.tex`
independently. Write the completed document to `/workspace/submission/main.tex`.
